-5. **Artifact discipline, every round.** Commit per-round reviewer outputs +
+2. **Artifact discipline, every round.** Commit per-round reviewer outputs +
   175	   **Delta dispatch.** The first round reviews the whole artifact; **every round
   176	   after the first is a delta review**. Carry the prior rounds' findings *and
   177	   their dispositions* into the reviewer task as a table, and scope the review to
   178	   the delta commit range since the previous round. Confirming a prior fix is one
   179	   line, not a re-litigation. The vocabulary those tables use — origin tags,
   180	   dispositions, finding ids — is defined once in
   181	   `references/system-reference.md`, "Iteration & disposition"; this section says
   182	   only who applies it and when.
   183	
   184	   **Harvest-at-dispatch (FS13).** Immediately after dispatching any design or PR
   185	   panel, record `panel.dispatched` and preserve the panel's artifacts with
   186	   `scripts/harvest-panel.sh --phase <panelPhase> --round <n> --from <asyncDir>`,
   187	   then `panel.consolidated` after adjudication — see
   188	   `references/system-reference.md` ("Lifecycle telemetry") for the event map.
   189	   An async dispatch's harvest is a point-in-time copy: **re-run the same
   190	   harvest once every child has reached a terminal state** so the preserved
   191	   artifacts carry final results, and harvest each replacement dispatch's own
   192	   `asyncDir` too rather than letting it vanish from the run store. `--round`
   193	   is a positive-integer destination label, not only a fix-wave counter: a
   194	   replacement dispatch takes its **own round number** — reusing the
   195	   original's would overwrite that snapshot. The telemetry events keep the
   196	   **logical wave number** regardless (a replacement's `panel.dispatched`
   197	   carries its original wave's round; see `references/system-reference.md`,
   198	   "Lifecycle telemetry"); only the harvest label advances, and the
   199	   label↔wave mapping is recorded in the wave's `consolidated.md`.
   200	
   201	3. **Consolidate**: collapse duplicates into one issue, keep cross-model agreement
   202	   as signal, preserve genuine disagreement. Consolidation also **mints each
   203	   finding's id** — `<PREFIX>-R<round>-<nn>`, unique within the run, the prefix
   204	   read off the panel phase by the glossary's closed mapping — and **tags each
   205	   consolidated row's origin**, `NEW` or `REOPENED(<id>)`. A reopen that fails the
   206	   glossary's evidence bar takes the disposition `barred`, recorded like any
   207	   other; it is not silently dropped and not re-argued.
   208	4. **Adjudicate**: for every high or medium finding, either incorporate it or
   209	   record a one-line reason for dismissal. Disclose the orchestrating model in the
   210	   consolidated file. Disputed high or medium findings are decided by the project's
   211	   human owner, who is the final adjudicator. Reviewer output is roughly eighty per
   212	   cent right and overreaches, so nothing is actioned blindly and nothing is
   213	   dismissed silently.
   214	
   215	   Escalate disputes to the human per the shared contract
   216	   (`references/system-reference.md`, "Presenting questions to the human") with
   217	   the PR delta: escalations reach the human **once per fix wave, after
   218	   consolidation, never streamed as reviewers return**, and arrive
   219	   **pre-adjudicated** as ratify/amend decisions — each escalated finding
   220	   carries its id, a one-line gist, the reviewers who raised it (cross-model
   221	   agreement is signal), and the agent's recommended disposition with its
   222	   reason. Only three cases escalate: **proposed dismissals of high or medium
   223	   findings**, anything touching a previously human-ratified residual-risk
   224	   boundary, and any finding that **contradicts an owner-ratified decision** —
   225	   which goes to the owner to decide and is never absorbed into the artifact nor
   226	   silently dismissed. Incorporating a finding is agreement and needs no
   227	   permission. Overflow past the cap usually means incorporate the cheap ones
   228	   rather than argue them. A **human-ratified dismissal binds forward**: record it in
   229	   `consolidated.md` with its human-ratified attribution and do not re-litigate
   230	   the same finding class in later waves or later sessions unless new evidence
   231	   emerges. The cross-session half of that rule needs a lookup, not memory:
   232	   **before adjudicating, search prior consolidated files under the configured
   233	   reviews home** (e.g. grep `<paths.reviews>/pr-*/consolidated.md` for
   234	   `ratif` — the broad stem, because records predating this attribution
   235	   convention word ratification differently) and treat any hit on the same
   236	   finding class as already adjudicated unless new evidence has emerged. The
   237	   **finding class** this rule keys on and the `defect class` of
   238	   `references/system-reference.md`, "Iteration & disposition", are two names for
   239	   one concept.
   240	
   241	   **Dismissal posture.** Dismissal is a real verdict, and a panel is not right
   242	   about everything: **two consecutive waves at 100% incorporation** is a
   243	   reportable smell, and the adjudicator says so to the human rather than
   244	   recording it as diligence. Propose dismissals, with one-line reasons, for
   245	   findings that would only be incorporated to avoid the argument.
   246	
   247	   **Trim the tail.** When a round yields no highs and at most one medium from a
   248	   single reviewer, fix it and re-dispatch **only that reviewer** for a delta
   249	   confirmation — or offer the human accept-without-re-dispatch. Do not run a
   250	   full multi-reviewer round to chase one medium.
   251	
   252	   **Sub-floor exemption.** The configured floor governs **full review rounds**.
   253	   A trim-the-tail confirmation is an exempt sub-floor dispatch, recorded as such
   254	   in that wave's `consolidated.md`, and is **not** a shortfall under
   255	   `review.onShortfall`.
   256	
   257	   **Backlog checkpoint.** `CARRY-TO-BACKLOG` is this phase's outbound carry: the
   258	   terminal destination for a finding that is valid but outside this change. The
   259	   PR gate is not passable while any `CARRY-TO-BACKLOG` lacks a filed issue id
   260	   recorded in `consolidated.md`. The PR gate is also the **inbound** checkpoint
   261	   for every carry minted anywhere in this run, whatever phase minted it: the
   262	   panel verifies each has landed at its named destination before the gate passes.
   263	5. **Stop** when no high or medium finding survives adjudication. Low findings are
   264	   recorded, not blocking. Termination is measured against surviving findings, so a
   265	   ruthless panel that always emits nits still converges.
   266	
   267	   **Round cap.** If the **4th** round still returns any high or medium finding,
   268	   no 5th round is dispatched. Diagnose instead, and put the diagnosis to the
   269	   human, who adjudicates between four bounded options: (a) genuine rev-1 defects
   270	   — continue; (b) churn generated by our own fix waves — restructure rather than
   271	   re-dispatch; (c) a design flaw — backward transition; (d) ratified dismissal of
   272	   the survivors. At `pr_review`, (d) is the only route to "move on", and the cap
   273	   never permits merging past a surviving high or medium finding.
   274	
   275	   **Artifact-inventory self-audit.** The diagnosis, and each gate presentation,
   276	   states the per-round inventory: round *n* ↔ its `consolidated.md` ↔ its
   277	   `panel.dispatched`/`panel.consolidated` events ↔ its harvest label.
   278	   Non-blocking — visibility, not enforcement.
   279	
   280	Save panel artifacts under `<configured paths.reviews>/<phase>-<feat>-<date>/`: one
   281	file per model, the shared `prompt.md`, and a `consolidated.md` carrying the
   282	adjudication and the orchestrating model. The `<phase>-review-<feat>-<date>`
   283	form (a `-review-` infix) is equally accepted and recommended going forward;
   284	the retro collector discovers both.
   285	
   286	> **Under your configuration:** whether a Plan panel and a Spec panel run at all
   287	> depends on the effective track and `review.design`; the PR panel runs on both
   288	> tracks. `review.code` (`panel` | `advisory` | `human` | `off`) sets the PR gate
   289	> strength. Read them; never assume `panel`.
   290	
   291	Run the local PR panel against the final committed branch, consolidate and
   292	adjudicate its findings in the durable internal review artifact under
   293	`docs/reviews/`, and repeat after each fix wave until no high or medium survives.
   294	This is the pre-PR sense check that the branch is a finished artefact; retain the
   295	artifact for future analysis, but do not add development findings to the PR body
   296	or post them as GitHub review comments.
   297	
   298	## 6. Refusal and backward-transition behaviour
   299	
   300	Merging with a high or medium finding that survived adjudication is forbidden.
   301	Backward transition to any earlier phase is always allowed when the panel exposes
   302	a design flaw. Only after the panel is clean, open the PR with the clean body.
   303	
   304	## 7. After-hook order and warning semantics
   305	
   306	Fire `hooks.pr.after` (and `hooks."*"`) after the PR opens: phase-specific first,
   307	then `*`. A failed `after` hook **warns** (recorded, never blocking).
   308	
   309	## 8. Completion evidence and next transition
   310	
   311	Completion evidence is a clean panel (no surviving high/medium), a passing
   312	`check-lifecycle`, and the opened PR with its clean body. **Completion is
   313	machine-checked, not narrated.** After the PR exists, do not state that the
   314	Implement/PR phase is "complete" or "PASS" without first running:
   315	
    75	repository at a named commit.
    76	
    77	> **Under your configuration:** `review.design` (`panel` | `advisory` | `human` |
    78	> `off`), possibly adjusted by per-track `overrides`, sets the spec gate. Read the
    79	> effective value from current `CONFIG.md` (or authoritative `sdlc.config.json`);
    80	> never assume `panel`. When `shape.separateSpec: false`, there is no separate
    81	> spec gate — the merged Plan+Spec artifact carries one design gate.
    82	
    83	When a panel runs it follows the shared panel run-shape owned by
    84	`references/phase-pr-review.md`, "Panels", via the `spec_review` phase; the
    85	reviewer prompt is `prompts/adversary-spec.prompt.md`. Reviewers are grounded
    86	in the code and must cite `file:line` for any framework claim.
    87	
    88	**Amending an approved Specification.** Which of the three amendment classes
    89	defined in `references/system-reference.md`, "Iteration & disposition", a change
    90	falls into decides whether this gate re-runs. Class **(a)** touches a shape
    91	already frozen, merged, or bound to: a backward transition, and the gate re-runs
    92	(§6). Class **(b)** refines this Specification while it is still unfrozen and
    93	unmerged: amend it in place, recording the trigger, the class, the disposition
    94	and the author — no new panel; where a later phase authors the amendment, this
    95	artifact still carries an in-place marker naming that record. Class **(c)**, a
    96	reviewer-grade contradiction surfacing later, is handled as a normal fix wave.
    97	
    98	**Inbound carries block this gate.** Before the Spec gate passes, verify that
    99	every `CARRY-TO-SPEC` minted at Plan has landed here, at the landing site its
   100	record names. An undischarged carry blocks the gate — the no-orphan rule of
   101	`references/system-reference.md`, "Iteration & disposition".
   102	
   103	> **Under your configuration:** a spec-panel finding that is valid but belongs to
   104	> decomposition takes the disposition `CARRY-TO-BUILD`, recorded with its landing
   105	> site — the build plan's spec-gap log. That destination is the next phase in the
   106	> *effective configured sequence*, so read that sequence from current
   107	> `CONFIG.md` (or authoritative `sdlc.config.json`) rather than assuming it.
   108	
   109	## 6. Refusal and backward-transition behaviour
   110	
   111	Standalone `sdlc:spec` refuses-with-redirect when adopted and no committed plan
   112	exists. Backward transition to Plan or Brainstorm is always allowed when the Spec
   113	reveals an upstream flaw. A class **(a)** amendment (§5) resolves here: it touches
   114	a shape already frozen, merged, or bound to, so it is a backward transition and
   115	the gate re-runs.
